findings.claim:
No case puts a child block across the 65,536-entry page boundary where the answer depends on reading the right page. The design leaves the read free to be 'a non-recursive loop over the page arrays'. A plausible optimisation looks up the page once at FirstChild and indexes `p.Kind[c & PageMask]` for the whole backward scan, which reads the wrong page past a boundary. Every planned test passes that bug:
- the sweeps' stores are about 2,000 entries, all within one page;
- in the criterion-5 shape, any index inside W's block is a NotListed folder, so a misread still yields true;
- in the criterion-3 shape, misreads on complete chains still yield false, and the one true chain's 240 entries cross a boundary with probability of about 0.4%.
Only an unmasked index throws.
